Done. All 5 tests in `tests/robot-name.rs` pass, and clippy is clean.

## Implementation (`src/lib.rs`)

- **`Robot { name: String }`** — each robot owns its name; `name()` returns a reference, so no allocation on read.
- **Global state** (required by the test setup): two `LazyLock<Mutex<…>>` statics — a `HashSet<String>` of every name ever handed out, and a shared PRNG. `generate_name()` locks both and loops until it draws an unused name, so uniqueness is guaranteed even under concurrent test threads.
- **Randomness without crates**: only `anyhow` was actually in the offline cargo cache, so I used a small xorshift64* PRNG seeded from `SystemTime`, generating two letters + three digits per name. The name space (26² × 10³ = 676,000) is large enough that random retries against the used-set will essentially never spin.

## A better API without global state

Yes — the global registry makes names unique *process-wide* and leaks retired names forever. The natural design is to make the registry an explicit value:

```rust
pub struct Factory {
    used_names: HashSet<String>,
    rng: Rng,
}

impl Factory {
    pub fn new() -> Self { … }

    pub fn create_robot(&mut self) -> Robot { … }

    pub fn reset(&mut self, robot: &mut Robot) { … }
}
```

The factory owns the set of issued names and hands out robots, so uniqueness is enforced by ownership rather than hidden global state — no `Mutex`, no `unsafe`, and it's trivially testable (each test gets its own `Factory`). The trade-off is that you must thread the `Factory` through to every call site, which is exactly why the exercise forces the global version.
